fore deciding whether to tackle the wc command implementation for rel01.0.\n\nSince I don't have the full PRD content in the project context, I'll work with what I can see in the source code comments. I can reference the PRD IDs that appear there—prd001-testutils, prd002-sys, and prd003-format—and use the specific requirements visible in the code to anchor my proposal for sys tests.\n\nLooking at the requirements, I need to group them within the 4-requirement limit per task. Terminal operations (R1), file metadata (R2), and signal handling (R3) form three distinct groups, so I'm on track there. Now I'm thinking through how to handle the remaining coverage—there's more ground to cover than just these three groups.\n\nI'm reconsidering whether to prioritize sys package tests or move forward with implementing cmd/wc/. The project's foundational code in pkg/sys/ already exists, but the tests are missing. However, the differential tests in cmd/ packages already validate behavior end-to-end, so maybe unit tests for the library itself aren't blocking progress. Let me step back and think about what's actually needed for the release—if format is already in progress and sys code exists, maybe I should focus on what's genuinely blocking the next milestone rather than filling every gap.\n\nLooking at rel01.0, I'd need to implement cmd/wc/ with both the main implementation and differential tests, but those should be split into separate tasks per the guidelines. The challenge is I don't have the PRD content for wc yet, though I can infer the file path follows the convention docs/specs/product-requirements/prd[NNN]-wc.yaml.\n\nFrom what I've seen, the PRDs are numbered sequentially (prd001-testutils, prd002-sys, prd003-format), so wc would likely be prd004 or higher, but I spotted a reference to prd019 which suggests there are more PRDs than I initially thought. The stitch agent will need the exact path to fetch the requirements, so I should look for the specific PRD number for wc